  (symbol (lib_id "Device:R") (at 49.53 132.08 180) (unit 1)
    (in_bom yes) (on_board yes) (dnp no)
    (uuid c16920c2-de68-565c-805a-2f645ed343e2)
    (property "Reference" "R30" (at 49.53 118.65 0)
      (effects (font (size 1.27 1.27)) )
    )
    (property "Value" "20" (at 52.07 145.51 0)
      (effects (font (size 1.27 1.27)) )
    )
    (property "Footprint" "Resistor_SMD:R_1206_3216Metric" (at 49.53 145.51 0)
      (effects (font (size 1.27 1.27)) hide)
    )
    (property "Datasheet" "~" (at 49.53 145.51 0)
      (effects (font (size 1.27 1.27)) hide)
    )
    (property "Note" "Series limit, and the value is set by the worst of the two pull-up cases. ISO 9141-2 wants the tester's dominant below 0.2*Vb = 2.4 V. Including the FET's roughly 18 ohm Rds(on) at a 3.3 V gate the leg is about 38 ohm, so against the ECU's 510 ohm alone the dominant is 12*38/548 = 0.83 V, and with the optional 750 ohm below also stuffed (304 ohm combined) it is 1.33 V. Both clear. 33 ohm would also have worked and 100 ohm would not, but 20 is what exists in 1206. A sustained short of K-line to battery while transmitting puts about 316 mA through the FET, over its 115 mA continuous rating -- it survives the pulse, not the fault. 1206 carries the pulse energy" (at 49.53 145.51 0)
      (effects (font (size 1.27 1.27)) hide)
    )
    (pin "1" (uuid 89be0ced-1cb0-5a00-89b6-0d292756e78c))
    (pin "2" (uuid 9bbb198c-fedd-57f8-98d5-cb71ad1c1e13))
